  "conclusions": [
    "All29 ResetWrangler findings map individually to exact baseline rows after only the declared APB clock and PCIe catcher-index transformations. This is a known endpoint/structure correspondence; no old A110 DCP was reopened. Counter duration changes11000/110MHz to5500/55MHz, preserving approximately100us, with width14 to13.",
    "The4 new PCIe CDC-7 destinations are identical to4 existing vendor fundamental-reset endpoints, previously launched by external PERST/POR. They are genuinely asynchronous reset paths, not four per-endpoint synchronizers.",
    "Actual post_opt common reset cone is LUT3 INIT F7 with I0=released,I1=qualified,I2=POR, followed by LUT2 INIT E OR with PCIE4CE4 CFGHOTRESETOUT. This matches vendor sys_or_hot_rst behavior. The receiving registers are one FDPE INIT1 in PCIe user-clock domain and3 FDCE INIT0 in pipe_clk domain.",
    "Vendor generated2020.2 core_top.v lines5186-5197,5573-5588 show direct asynchronous sys_or_hot_rst use. Lines5664-5669 connect sys_reset and combine hot reset. Lines5690-5707 implement2-stage xpm_cdc_async_rst plus user_reset output register for synchronous release. The same2 FDPE ASYNC_REG stages and direct D connections are present in the actual DCP.",
    "No new startup or AXI gate Critical/Warning appears in ordinary or all-checks CDC output. This is not proof from absence: related55/110 paths are treated as timed related clocks; the separate structural and66 targeted routed timing checks remain mandatory.",
    "A100/oldA110 history is differential context, not an independent blanket CDC waiver. All proposed dispositions must be re-evaluated against final routed reports and DCP."
  ],
  "official_sources": [
    {
      "url": "https://docs.amd.com/r/en-US/pg213-pcie4-ultrascale-plus/Resets",
      "version": "PG213 v1.3,2026-07-31",
      "paraphrase": "The fundamental reset input is asynchronous and resets the core and transceivers; hold it until the PCIe reference clock is stable. User reset deassertion is synchronized to user_clk."
    },
    {
      "url": "https://docs.amd.com/r/en-US/pg213-pcie4-ultrascale-plus/Clock-and-Reset-Interface",
      "version": "PG213 v1.3,2026-07-31",
      "paraphrase": "sys_reset accepts an asynchronous active-low fundamental reset. The PCIe-derived user clock need not run continuously during reset."
    },
    {
      "url": "https://docs.amd.com/r/en-US/pg195-pcie-dma/XDMA-Global-Ports",
      "version": "PG195 v4.2,2026-07-22",
      "paraphrase": "sys_rst_n is the connector reset input; axi_aresetn is synchronized to axi_aclk for downstream AXI logic."
    }
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Official web manuals are newer than installed Vivado2020.2; exact installed vendor HDL and actual DCP were inspected alongside their reset-interface semantics.",
    "No claim is made that a board100MHz monitor proves PCIe reference-clock stability. External PERST/refclk system contract remains required.",
    "No routed timing, minimum pulse width, recovery/removal, physical clock uncertainty, or final CDC acceptance is provided by this pre-route diagnostic.",
    "The four internal vendor endpoints are directly asynchronously reset; their supported-interface classification must not be generalized to custom unsynchronized data/reset crossings.",
    "Post-opt hierarchy pins named sys_rst_n/sys_reset may alias an optimized inverted-reset net. Primitive endpoints and their LUT truth/polarities, rather than retained boundary names alone, establish the actual reset meaning."
  ]
}
